regress\sgi_de_width_probe.py:193:def printed32(t, reg):
regress\sgi_de_width_probe.py-194-    """The low 32 bits of a register loaded by `lw`.
regress\sgi_de_width_probe.py-195-
regress\sgi_de_width_probe.py-196-    *** lw SIGN-EXTENDS.  A 32-bit read of 0xcafebabe lands in the 64-bit
regress\sgi_de_width_probe.py-197-    register as 0xffffffffcafebabe, and a row comparing the raw string then
regress\sgi_de_width_probe.py-198-    FAILS on MIPS semantics rather than on device behaviour.  That bit this
regress\sgi_de_width_probe.py-199-    detector on its first run -- 21/22 with the device entirely correct --
regress\sgi_de_width_probe.py-200-    and the two neighbouring accept rows passed only because their fixtures
regress\sgi_de_width_probe.py-201-    happen to have bit 31 CLEAR. ***
regress\sgi_de_width_probe.py-202-
regress\sgi_de_width_probe.py-203-    Masking rather than choosing a friendlier constant is deliberate: it
regress\sgi_de_width_probe.py-204-    makes the row correct BY CONSTRUCTION for any value, instead of correct
regress\sgi_de_width_probe.py-205-    by an accident of the fixture that the next person to edit it cannot
regress\sgi_de_width_probe.py-206-    see.  Nothing is lost -- for a `lw` the upper 32 bits carry no device
regress\sgi_de_width_probe.py-207-    data, only the sign extension.
regress\sgi_de_width_probe.py-208-    """
regress\sgi_de_width_probe.py-209-    v = printed(t, reg)
